  <h2 id="iaflow">How a control loop works in plain words</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Part</th>
          <th>Job</th>
          <th>Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Sensor</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Job">Measures something such as level or temperature</td>
          <td data-label="Example">Level sensor in a tank</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Controller</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Job">Decides what to do with the reading</td>
          <td data-label="Example">PLC with a logic program</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Actuator</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Job">Acts on the process</td>
          <td data-label="Example">Pump or valve</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Display and logging</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Job">Shows state and records data</td>
          <td data-label="Example">SCADA screen</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Part"><strong>Safety layer</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Job">Stops the process if limits are crossed</td>
          <td data-label="Example">Emergency stop and alarms</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every automation task fits this loop. Practise describing any machine in these five parts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask a technician to walk you through one loop on a real machine.
  </p>

